<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yves d'Évreux</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1577–c. 1632</span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Yves d'<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/evreux">Évreux</a> was a French Capuchin friar who served in the colony of Equinoctial <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> on the island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maranhao">Maranhão</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> from 1612. His account of the colony and of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tupinamba">Tupinambá</a> continues that of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claude-dabbeville">Claude d'Abbeville</a>, but its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a> was suppressed in 1615 after France made peace with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, and it was only republished in 1864. It is a key source on the Tupinambá.</p></div>
